1.18.1: the serial tail - raw decode and JPEG encode in bands

1.18.0 threaded the pipeline; the raw decoder and the JPEG writer were
still single-threaded and had become the largest part of a raw render.

Raw: tiles decode in parallel (one scratch buffer per band), the
lossless-JPEG Huffman walk resolves through a 9-bit lookup, the bit
reader counts padding explicitly so a stream's last code is never
mistaken for an overrun, and the linearization, normalize, demosaic,
white-balance/matrix and orientation passes all run in row bands. The
144 MB intermediate buffer is gone: the image buffer is the work
buffer.

JPEG: a baseline encoder of our own (src/core/jpegenc.c) replaces
stb_image_write's. Same arithmetic - the AAN float DCT, stb's quality
scaling, the Annex K tables, 4:4:4 - so it reproduces stb's output
pixel for pixel on decode; what differs is structure: the image is
cut into bands of 8 MCU rows, each entropy-coded on its own thread
with its own DC predictors, joined with restart markers (DRI). The
band height is fixed, so the file is the same bytes at any thread
count; test_threads.sh now checks raw decode and JPEG output too. The
8-bit quantization before encode is banded as well.

12 MP ProRAW-like DNG through 35mm-negative with a LUT, 2-core
sandbox: 9.4 s (1.17.1) -> 4.4 s (1 thread) -> 2.9 s (2 threads); the
decode alone 0.72 -> 0.59 s on a single-tile file.

.gitignore: *.DNG, *.NEF, *.xmp and a root .cube no longer dirty a
checkout (the synthetic tests/raw files stay un-ignored below them;
release.sh keeps that order on older checkouts).
